## ABSTRACT

The global patent system, an institution forged in the scarcity economy of the Industrial Revolution, confronts a foundational crisis. The assumptions underlying centuries of intellectual property doctrine — that invention is rare, that novelty assessment demands expert human effort, and that 20-year uniform protection terms are economically rational — have been systematically invalidated by the emergence of large-scale artificial intelligence. According to the World Intellectual Property Organization, AI patent filings have soared dramatically in recent years, with generative AI patents alone increasing by over 800% since 2017. [Greyb Insights](https://insights.greyb.com/artificial-intelligence-patent-landscape/) Global patent filings reached 3.7 million in 2024, marking a 4.9% jump over 2023, with China alone accounting for roughly 70% of approximately 54,000 GenAI patent families filed between 2014 and 2023. [Aicerts News](https://www.aicerts.ai/news/wipo-patent-growth-surges-in-ict-filing-records/) The system built to manage the scarcity of invention is now straining under the abundance of it.

This dissertation proposes the **AI-Native Patent Institution (AIPI)** — a comprehensive theoretical framework for redesigning intellectual property governance from first principles for a civilization in which machine intelligence augments or partially replaces human inventive capacity. The AIPI is grounded in four theoretical pillars: (1) a **Global Knowledge Graph (GKG)** serving as the canonical prior art substrate, (2) a **Multi-Dimensional Evaluation Protocol (MDEP)** providing computational novelty, utility, reproducibility, impact, and risk indices, (3) a **Contribution Stratification Doctrine (CSD)** establishing formal tiers of human-AI inventive contribution with corresponding ownership and disclosure obligations, and (4) an **Adaptive Protection Calculus (APC)** linking patent duration empirically to sectoral innovation velocity. The dissertation further proposes an **Innovation Commons** mechanism and a **Public Benefit Test** as mandatory gatekeeping instruments, resolving the core tension between innovation incentive and monopolistic hoarding that AI-abundance makes acute.

**Keywords:** Artificial intelligence, patent law, intellectual property reform, knowledge graph, novelty assessment, AI inventorship, DABUS, USPTO, WIPO, adaptive patent duration, innovation commons, computational governance.

