## CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION — THE SCARCITY ASSUMPTION COLLAPSES

### 1.1 The Founding Premise

Every institution is a crystallized theory about the world. The modern patent system, tracing its lineage to the English Statute of Monopolies of 1624, the French system of 1791, and the American Patent Act of 1790, crystallized a theory in which inventive capacity was rare, costly, and required extraordinary human ingenuity. The purpose of a temporary monopoly — the bargain at the heart of patent law — was precisely to compensate for this rarity: society grants exclusive rights in exchange for public disclosure, because without such incentive, the high cost of invention would deter inventors entirely.

This founding theory was coherent for its time and remained largely coherent through the Industrial Revolution, the rise of corporate R&D in the twentieth century, and even through the early decades of the software revolution. The marginal cost of producing knowledge remained high enough, and the cognitive demands of genuine invention remained substantial enough, that the scarcity assumption held in practice even where it could be questioned in principle.

This assumption is now collapsing.

### 1.2 The Velocity Crisis

The collapse is empirical, not merely theoretical. WIPO data shows that generative AI-related patent applications grew by more than 100% in 2024–2025, with combined AI patent applications from China, the United States, and Europe accounting for over 85% of global AI patent volume. [Cnipa](https://cnipa.ai/blog/patent-technology-trends-2026) In 2023 alone, there were 14,080 GenAI patent family publications, compared to almost 230,000 AI patent family publications in total. [WIPO](https://www.wipo.int/web-publications/patent-landscape-report-generative-artificial-intelligence-genai/assets/62504/Generative%20AI%20-%20PLR%20EN_WEB2.pdf) Systems capable of generating pharmaceutical compounds, engineering designs, software algorithms, material formulations, and process innovations are no longer experimental — they are deployed at scale, daily, across every major R&D organization on earth.

This velocity creates what this dissertation terms the **Inventive Flood**: a regime in which the rate of candidate patent applications structurally exceeds the examination capacity of patent offices operating under manual-review assumptions. The consequence is not merely administrative congestion. It is doctrinal erosion: when novelty cannot be evaluated rigorously because of throughput constraints, the social bargain underlying patent law — exclusive rights in exchange for genuinely useful, non-obvious disclosure — silently breaks down.

### 1.3 The Institutional Response Gap

The legal system's response to this crisis has been fragmented, defensive, and jurisdictionally incoherent. The US Patent and Trademark Office's Revised Inventorship Guidance for AI-Assisted Inventions, issued on 28 November 2025 and effective immediately, reaffirms that only natural persons can be inventors, treats AI systems as mere instruments analogous to other inventor support tools such as software and laboratory equipment, and applies the traditional, fact-intensive US 'conception' test uniformly to all inventions without any separate or modified rules for AI assistance. [Oxford Academic](https://academic.oup.com/jiplp/advance-article/doi/10.1093/jiplp/jpag021/8528856)

While this guidance achieves formal clarity, it simultaneously evades the deeper crisis. Treating a system that autonomously generates a novel pharmaceutical scaffold as equivalent to "laboratory equipment" — in the same category as a centrifuge or a mass spectrometer — is a legal fiction that clarifies ownership while obscuring epistemic reality. The 2025 guidance leaves unresolved the central tension created by AI: under the 2024 approach, an inventor could satisfy the inventorship requirement so long as the human made a significant contribution to the claim as a whole, even if the AI supplied certain claim limitations. [BitLaw](https://www.bitlaw.com/ai/AI-inventors.html)

The result is a system in which patent law formally preserves human inventorship while increasingly accommodating machine generation, without a principled framework for where to draw the line, how to calibrate protection terms, or how to prevent AI-enabled patent flooding from entrenching monopoly power in the hands of those who can afford large-scale AI deployment.

### 1.4 Research Questions

This dissertation addresses five interrelated research questions:

RQ1: What theoretical foundations justify the reconstitution of patent governance institutions for an era of abundant AI-generated invention, and what normative principles should guide the design of successor institutions?

RQ2: How can computational methods be formally specified to augment — without replacing — human judgment in novelty, utility, and impact assessment, particularly at scale?

RQ3: What legal doctrine should govern the relationship between human inventive contribution and AI generative output, across a continuous spectrum from pure human invention to substantially autonomous machine generation?

RQ4: How should patent protection duration be made responsive to empirically measurable sectoral innovation velocity, and what institutional mechanisms enforce such adaptivity?

RQ5: How can an innovation commons mechanism be designed that structurally addresses the AI-era risk of monopolistic hoarding of machine-generated knowledge, without eliminating legitimate innovation incentives?

### 1.5 Contributions

This dissertation makes the following original theoretical contributions:

C1: A first-principles derivation of the AIPI institutional architecture grounded in the economics of non-rival knowledge goods and the Jevons Paradox of AI-driven inventive abundance.

C2: A formal mathematical specification of the Global Knowledge Graph (GKG) as a dynamic prior art substrate, including a vector-space novelty distance metric and its theoretical properties.

C3: A Multi-Dimensional Evaluation Protocol (MDEP) with formally specified index functions for novelty, utility, reproducibility, impact, and risk, together with an aggregation framework respecting the non-commensurability of these dimensions.

C4: A Contribution Stratification Doctrine (CSD) providing a principled legal taxonomy of human-AI inventive relationships, grounded in comparative analysis of DABUS jurisprudence across eight global jurisdictions as of mid-2026.

C5: An Adaptive Protection Calculus (APC) deriving sector-specific patent duration from first-principles modeling of innovation cycles, R&D cost structures, and social welfare optimization.

C6: A constitutional-level governance architecture maintaining human sovereignty over intellectual property rights while permitting deep AI integration into evaluation processes.

