## CHAPTER 2: HISTORICAL FOUNDATIONS AND THE CRISIS OF DOCTRINE

### 2.1 The Evolution of the Patent Bargain

Patent law's foundational bargain — monopoly in exchange for disclosure — has survived three centuries of technological transformation by maintaining formal continuity while absorbing institutional change. The Statute of Monopolies outlawed royal grants of monopoly except for inventions that were new, useful, and limited in time. The Paris Convention of 1883 established the first international coordination mechanism. The TRIPS Agreement of 1994 embedded patent protection in the global trading order, mandating minimum 20-year protection terms for all member states of the World Trade Organization.

Throughout these transformations, the bargain's internal logic held: invention was costly, novelty was determinable by expert human review against a manageable prior art corpus, and a uniform protection term of approximately 20 years was a defensible approximation of what was needed to recoup R&D investment across major technology sectors.

### 2.2 The Examination Crisis

The system's examination infrastructure was built for a world of approximately 50,000–100,000 annual global filings. It now operates in a world of 3.7 million annual filings. The consequences are compounding. The USPTO is unable to acquire all of the available databases of prior art needed to accurately search public domain material and is unable to provide its patent examiners with the time they need to properly examine the applications they receive — a structural funding and capacity problem that leads to greater uncertainty and more unnecessary litigation. [WIPO](https://www.wipo.int/en/web/wipo-magazine/articles/patent-trolls-friend-or-foe-38812)

The backlog is not an administrative inconvenience. It is a systemic failure of the social bargain. Patents granted without rigorous novelty review are not genuine monopoly grants in exchange for disclosure; they are lottery tickets issued by an overloaded bureaucracy, whose validity is only tested if a well-resourced challenger initiates expensive litigation.

### 2.3 The Patent Thicket Problem and AI Amplification

The phenomenon of **patent thickets** — dense networks of overlapping intellectual property rights that impede follow-on innovation — was identified as a structural pathology of the patent system long before AI. Patent thickets have potential antitrust implications, as illustrated in In re Humira, where pharmaceutical company AbbVie's thicket of over 100 patents on Humira extended the company's monopoly past the twenty-year limit and effectively prevented a generic version of the drug from entering the market, keeping prices inflated to upwards of $72,000 a year. [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patent_thicket)

AI dramatically amplifies the patent thicket risk. A large AI system can generate hundreds or thousands of candidate claims in the time a human inventor would generate one, enabling strategic patent flooding — the deliberate accumulation of dense claim portfolios around a technology space to foreclose competitive entry — at costs that were previously prohibitive. The social cost of thickets scales super-linearly with filing volume, making the combination of AI generation capacity and inadequate examination a particularly dangerous institutional failure mode.

### 2.4 The Non-Obviousness Standard Under Algorithmic Pressure

The non-obviousness standard — requiring that an invention not be obvious to a "person having ordinary skill in the art" at the time of filing — is perhaps the most conceptually stressed element of existing doctrine in the AI era. If an AI system capable of expert-level reasoning across a technical domain is used as an invention tool, what constitutes the relevant "ordinary skill"? Does the standard adjust upward to reflect AI-augmented capability, effectively raising the bar for human inventors? Or does it remain anchored to unaugmented human cognition, creating an anachronistic standard that increasingly departs from the actual frontier of technical possibility?

Recent research on LLMs and patent regulation reveals that models exhibit high embedding similarity between legal terms like "novel," "inventive," "nonobvious," and even "obvious" — with similarity scores between "novel" and "inventive" reaching 0.73 — suggesting that even advanced AI models may not create sufficient semantic distance between otherwise distinct legal concepts. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/html/2507.10576v1) This semantic conflation in AI models presents a deep challenge to any naive automation of non-obviousness assessment.

These doctrinal pressures — examination overload, amplified patent thickets, conceptually unstable non-obviousness standards — constitute the crisis of doctrine that the AIPI is designed to resolve.

