## CHAPTER 3: THE ECONOMICS OF KNOWLEDGE ABUNDANCE

### 3.1 The Non-Rivalry of Ideas

Economic analysis of knowledge goods begins with their fundamental non-rivalry: unlike physical commodities, an idea consumed by one party is not thereby unavailable to others. In economics, non-rivalry refers to goods whose consumption by one individual does not reduce their availability to others, such as ideas, information, and digital content. However, copyright laws, patents, and other forms of intellectual property protection make these naturally abundant resources artificially scarce, allowing creators and inventors to control access and monetize their work — a system that provides economic incentives for innovation but creates artificial scarcity leading to inefficiencies, including life-saving medical treatments priced out of reach for many despite negligible production costs. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.13168)

The economic rationale for patents is thus fundamentally remedial: they impose artificial scarcity on a non-rival good in order to internalize what would otherwise be an externality, ensuring that inventors can recoup sunk costs that would otherwise make invention privately irrational even when it is socially valuable. The welfare cost of this artificial scarcity — the deadweight loss from monopoly pricing and the innovation-retarding effects of patent thickets — is justified by the welfare gain from incentivized invention.

### 3.2 The Jevons Paradox of AI Invention

This remedial logic is now being disrupted by an empirical phenomenon that this dissertation terms the **AI Invention Jevons Paradox**. The classical Jevons Paradox, first articulated for coal consumption in 1865, holds that efficiency improvements in resource use lead not to reduced consumption but to increased demand. As AI gets more efficient and accessible, use is projected to skyrocket, turning it into a commodity we just can't get enough of — with implications for every domain in which AI reduces the cost of a cognitive task. [Northeastern Global News](https://news.northeastern.edu/2025/02/07/jevons-paradox-ai-future/)

Applied to invention: as AI reduces the marginal cost of generating a candidate invention toward zero, the total volume of candidate inventions explodes, not contracts. The system's response to the scarcity that justified artificial exclusivity — by creating more and more artificial exclusivity — therefore risks producing a self-reinforcing cycle of monopoly accumulation that is entirely decoupled from social innovation productivity.

Let $I_{human}(t)$ denote the rate of human-generated inventions at time $t$, and $I_{AI}(t)$ the rate of AI-generated inventions. Define the **social value** of each invention as $v_i$, and the **monopoly rent** captured through patent exclusivity as $\mu_i$. The classical welfare analysis requires:

$$\sum_i (\mu_i - DWL_i) > 0$$

where $DWL_i$ is the deadweight loss from monopoly exclusivity on invention $i$. This inequality was the historical justification for the patent system. As $I_{AI}(t) \rightarrow \infty$ while the rate of socially valuable inventions $V(t) = \sum_i v_i$ grows more slowly (because many AI-generated "inventions" are trivially obvious to AI systems even if novel under current doctrine), the system generates:

$$\sum_i DWL_i \gg \sum_i \mu_i^{innovation}$$

The patent system transitions from welfare-enhancing to welfare-reducing. This is the fundamental economic argument for the AIPI.

### 3.3 Concentration Risk and Market Power

A major challenge is that AI can exacerbate the market dominance of large "superstar" companies, reflected in declining business dynamism and increasing market power; research documents that technology-driven productivity gains have been offset by raising market power, leading to net welfare loss. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0954349X24001826) In the patent domain, this concentration risk is acute: large technology and pharmaceutical companies deploying AI at scale can generate and file patents at rates impossible for smaller competitors, effectively weaponizing the patent system — whose filing infrastructure makes no distinction between a human invention and a machine-generated one — as a structural barrier to competitive entry.

The AIPI must therefore address not only the epistemic challenge of evaluating AI-generated novelty, but the political economy challenge of preventing AI-enabled patent accumulation from becoming a mechanism of competitive exclusion at civilizational scale.

