## CHAPTER 4: THE GLOBAL LEGAL LANDSCAPE — AI INVENTORSHIP DOCTRINE IN 2025–2026

### 4.1 The DABUS Jurisprudence: A Global Survey

The most significant global test case for AI inventorship doctrine has been the series of proceedings arising from Stephen Thaler's attempt to register his AI system, DABUS (Device for the Autonomous Bootstrapping of Unified Sentience), as the legal inventor of two inventions: a fractal food container and an emergency attention-drawing light.

The global jurisprudence reached near-total convergence on the formal outcome: AI cannot be a legal inventor. However, the reasoning reveals important divergences that are theoretically significant for AIPI design.

**United States (Federal Circuit, August 2022; USPTO Revised Guidance, November 2025):** The 2025 Inventorship Guidance reaffirms that only natural persons can be inventors, treats AI systems as mere instruments analogous to laboratory equipment, and applies the traditional, fact-intensive US 'conception' test uniformly to all inventions, eliminating Pannu-factor analysis when only one natural person is involved. [Oxford Academic](https://academic.oup.com/jiplp/advance-article/doi/10.1093/jiplp/jpag021/8528856)

**United Kingdom (Supreme Court, 2023; High Court confirmation, September 2025):** The UK Supreme Court held that DABUS did not meet the criteria to be an inventor, while Lord Kitchin stated that, had Dr. Thaler asserted instead that "he was the inventor and used DABUS as a highly sophisticated tool, the outcome of these proceedings might well have been different." [Herbert Smith Freehills Kramer](https://www.hsfkramer.com/notes/ip/2025-09/thalers-ai-patent-application-confirmed-deemed-withdrawn-by-high-court)

**Germany (Bundesgerichtshof, June 2024):** The Federal Court of Justice reinforced the principle that only natural persons can be named as inventors, while emphasizing that human influence in AI-generated inventions does not need to be inventive or substantial — what matters is the presence of a human influence in the invention process. [Norton Rose Fulbright](https://www.nortonrosefulbright.com/en/knowledge/publications/7de4a9ba/germany-ai-cannot-be-named-as-inventor-insights-from-the-bundesgerichtshofs-dabus-decision)

**Japan (IP High Court, January 2025):** Japan's IP High Court substantially rejected recognition of DABUS as an inventor, after which Japan's Cabinet Office published the Intellectual Property Strategic Program 2025, addressing inventorship and the qualification of generative AI as a patent applicant, and proposing prompt legislative responses recognizing Japan's commitment to proactive engagement in international IP frameworks. [AIPPI](https://www.aippi.org/news/ai-as-an-inventor-of-patents-ip-high-court-judgment-and-the-2025-ip-strategic-program/)

**Canada (Patent Appeal Board, June 2025):** The Patent Appeal Board definitively rejected AI inventorship under current Canadian law, confirming that inventorship is limited to natural persons and that AI cannot assign or hold rights. [Gilbertslaw](https://www.gilbertslaw.ca/insights/2025/07/canadian-patent-appeal-board-says-ai-cannot-be-an-inventor/)

**European Patent Office:** The EPO rejected Thaler's applications on the grounds that the European Patent Convention requires the inventor to be a natural person with legal capacity — not merely an assumption on which the EPC was drafted, but the ordinary meaning of the term "inventor." [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DABUS)

**South Africa** was the singular exception, granting Thaler's application through a depository system checking only formal requirements — an aberration that illustrates the global fragmentation problem rather than a genuine alternative doctrinal approach.

### 4.2 The Unresolved Tension

While the formal convergence on "only natural persons can be inventors" appears decisive, it resolves a surface question while leaving the deeper one untouched. The difficulty is that the 2025 USPTO guidance rejects the only explicit framework for evaluating joint AI-human contribution — the Pannu-based analysis — and in its place recites first principles about conception without exploring which standard should govern when AI generates only some portion of a claimed invention, leaving unresolved the central tension created by AI. [BitLaw](https://www.bitlaw.com/ai/AI-inventors.html)

This tension is not merely doctrinal. It is epistemic. The question "who conceived the invention?" presupposes that conception is a locatable event with a determinable agent. In many AI-assisted inventions, conception is a distributed process: a human formulates a problem, an AI explores a vast hypothesis space, the human selects from machine-generated candidates using scientific judgment, and further AI processing refines the selected candidate. No element of this process individually satisfies what doctrine demands, yet the composite process is genuinely inventive in the socially relevant sense.

### 4.3 The Contribution Spectrum

Rather than a binary human/machine distinction, the AIPI grounds its legal framework in a **Contribution Spectrum** — a continuous variable $\lambda \in [0, 1]$ representing the fraction of creative contribution attributable to identified human inventors, formally defined in Chapter 7. The global jurisprudence has established that $\lambda = 0$ (pure machine invention with no human contribution beyond ownership) is legally insufficient. The AIPI proposes a principled framework for all values of $\lambda \in (0, 1]$.

