## CHAPTER 5: THE GLOBAL KNOWLEDGE GRAPH — ARCHITECTURE AND FORMAL SPECIFICATION

### 5.1 Motivation

The cornerstone of the AIPI's operational infrastructure is the **Global Knowledge Graph (GKG)** — a continuously updated, semantically structured representation of the entirety of human and AI-generated technical knowledge, serving as the canonical substrate for novelty assessment. The GKG transforms prior art from a collection of heterogeneous documents to be manually searched into a structured semantic space in which distances are computable and novelty is measurable.

### 5.2 Formal Definition

**Definition 5.1 (Global Knowledge Graph).** The GKG is a directed weighted hypergraph $\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}, \phi, \psi)$ where:

- $\mathcal{V}$ is the set of knowledge nodes, each representing a distinct technical concept, claim element, physical principle, chemical compound, algorithm, or biological entity.
- $\mathcal{E} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{V}) \times \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{V})$ is the set of hyperedges encoding relationships among nodes, including: taxonomic subsumption, causal dependency, citation linkage, experimental confirmation, structural similarity, and functional equivalence.
- $\phi: \mathcal{V} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^d$ is an embedding function mapping each knowledge node to a dense vector in $d$-dimensional embedding space, computed by a foundation model trained on the corpus of patents, scientific publications, technical standards, and registered disclosures.
- $\psi: \mathcal{E} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^+$ is an edge weight function encoding the strength and reliability of each relationship, updated according to citation evidence, experimental replication, and temporal decay.

**Definition 5.2 (Novelty Distance).** For an invention $I$ and a knowledge node $v \in \mathcal{V}$, the point novelty distance is:

$$\delta(I, v) = \| \phi(I) - \phi(v) \|_2$$

The **aggregate novelty distance** of $I$ with respect to the GKG is:

$$\Delta(I) = \min_{v \in \mathcal{N}_k(I)} \delta(I, v)$$

where $\mathcal{N}_k(I)$ is the set of $k$-nearest neighbors of $I$ in embedding space (the $k$ most similar existing knowledge nodes), and $\Delta(I)$ measures the distance to the closest prior art cluster.

**Definition 5.3 (Novelty Index).** The Novelty Index $NI(I) \in [0, 1]$ is defined as:

$$NI(I) = 1 - \exp\left(-\alpha \cdot \Delta(I)\right)$$

where $\alpha > 0$ is a scaling constant calibrated such that $NI = 0.5$ corresponds to the median novelty of previously granted patents in the relevant technology class. $NI \rightarrow 1$ as $I$ becomes maximally distant from all prior art; $NI \rightarrow 0$ as $I$ collapses onto existing knowledge nodes.

### 5.3 GKG Corpus Composition

The GKG shall ingest and maintain:

(i) All granted patents globally (currently exceeding 150 million documents via PATENTSCOPE), continuously updated.
(ii) Scientific publications from major indexed repositories (arXiv, PubMed, IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, Web of Science).
(iii) Technical standards from ISO, IEC, IEEE, IETF, and national standards bodies.
(iv) Open-source software codebases registered with the Open Source Initiative, with semantic code embeddings.
(v) Preprints and working papers from major preprint servers, with temporal provenance metadata.
(vi) Voluntary registrations of technical disclosures made through the Innovation Commons mechanism (Chapter 9).

**Proposition 5.1.** The GKG, under the above corpus definition, satisfies the legal requirement of "all prior art accessible to a person having ordinary skill in the art" under the prevailing non-obviousness standard if and only if the corpus includes all documents constructively accessible through reasonable professional diligence as of the priority date. This condition is not guaranteed by corpus completeness alone and requires systematic ingestion of non-English, non-indexed, and gray literature sources.

**Proof sketch:** A document $d$ constitutes prior art under 35 U.S.C. § 102 if it is a printed publication publicly available before the effective filing date. "Publicly available" encompasses all languages and all geographies. The GKG satisfies the condition if and only if every such publication is ingested. Partial corpus coverage creates systematic novelty assessment bias toward technology areas with stronger formal publication cultures. QED.

This proposition establishes a **completeness obligation** for the GKG that is non-trivial and requires explicit institutional resourcing, particularly for non-Western and non-English technical literature.

### 5.4 Temporal Dynamics and Knowledge Graph Decay

Technical knowledge is not static. Prior art that was novel at filing may become standard practice by the time of examination or enforcement. The GKG must therefore incorporate **temporal confidence weighting**:

$$\phi_t(v) = \phi_0(v) \cdot e^{-\gamma(t - t_0)}$$

where $\phi_0(v)$ is the initial embedding at ingestion time $t_0$, and $\gamma > 0$ is a technology-class-specific decay constant reflecting the rate at which technical fields evolve. Software-adjacent knowledge nodes carry higher $\gamma$ than pharmaceutical or materials science nodes, reflecting faster obsolescence cycles.

### 5.5 Multimodal Extension

Prior art search must identify similarities between new inventions and existing technologies, ensuring inventions satisfy novelty and non-obviousness requirements during patent drafting, examination, and infringement analysis, and this process inherently involves challenges posed by the abstract visual features of patent images. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.19360)

The GKG extends beyond textual embeddings to incorporate multimodal representations: chemical structural diagrams (via graph neural networks on molecular topology), engineering drawings (via vision transformers), code (via code-specific embedding models), and biological sequences (via protein and nucleotide language models). Each modality contributes a distinct embedding to a fused multimodal representation $\phi^{MM}(I) \in \mathbb{R}^{d \cdot M}$ where $M$ is the number of modalities.

