## CHAPTER 12: CONCLUSION

### 12.1 The Institutional Imperative

This dissertation has argued that the global patent system faces not an administrative challenge but a civilizational one. The assumptions embedded in 300 years of intellectual property doctrine — scarcity of invention, determinability of novelty by expert human review, uniform protection terms calibrated to industrial-era investment horizons — have been systematically undermined by the emergence of large-scale artificial intelligence.

The evidence is empirical and accelerating. Generative AI patents have increased by over 800% since 2017 [Greyb Insights](https://insights.greyb.com/artificial-intelligence-patent-landscape/) , and WIPO data shows that generative AI-related patent applications grew by more than 100% in 2024–2025 alone. [Cnipa](https://cnipa.ai/blog/patent-technology-trends-2026) AI risks exacerbating market dominance of large superstar companies, with technology-driven productivity gains potentially offset by rising market power leading to net welfare loss. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0954349X24001826) The patent system, left unreformed, does not solve these problems — it amplifies them.

The AI-Native Patent Institution proposes a response equal to the magnitude of the challenge. It does not propose cosmetic updates to examination procedures or isolated refinements to the non-obviousness standard. It proposes a principled redesign of the institution itself: its epistemic infrastructure (the GKG), its evaluation methodology (the MDEP), its legal doctrine (the CSD), its economic calibration (the APC), and its social accountability mechanisms (the Public Benefit Test and the Innovation Commons).

### 12.2 The Human Sovereignty Thesis

This dissertation grounds all AI integration in a principled commitment to human sovereignty over intellectual property rights. This commitment is not sentimental — it is structural. Formal analysis (Theorem 6.1) establishes that no computable decision function can replicate human adjudication across all patent decisions, because legally relevant factors include ethically non-computable contextual considerations. The AIPI's architecture therefore deploys AI as a precision instrument for measurable quantities — novelty distances, utility estimates, reproducibility assessments — while preserving human judgment for the contextual, precedential, and normative dimensions of patent adjudication that no algorithm can adequately represent.

Human sovereignty in intellectual property governance is also an institutional prerequisite for legitimacy. Laws governing property rights derive their authority from democratic processes and are accountable to the communities they govern. An IP institution whose core decisions are made by AI systems without meaningful human review would lack the democratic grounding that gives intellectual property law its claim on compliance.

### 12.3 The Larger Vision

The institutions of the industrial era managed the scarcity of inventive capacity. The institutions of the AI era must manage its abundance — ensuring that the flood of machine-generated technical knowledge serves human flourishing rather than entrenching the power of those who own the most capable machines.

The AIPI's ultimate measure of success is not administrative efficiency, though the GKG and MDEP substantially improve it. It is not doctrinal coherence, though the CSD provides it. It is the degree to which the global intellectual property system, reconstituted for an era of abundant intelligence, actually accelerates the beneficial deployment of technology across the full breadth of humanity — including, critically, those in the Global South who are systematically underrepresented in existing filing data and who bear the greatest cost of monopoly pricing on essential technologies.

If the cost of invention is tending toward zero, the cost of access should tend there too. The AIPI is designed to ensure that the abundance AI creates is not captured as monopoly rent, but distributed as civilizational progress.

